Current cluster context|kubectl config current-context|Prints the selected kubeconfig context.|Confirm the cluster identity before interacting with workloads.|The context name is local configuration; inspect its cluster and namespace if ambiguous.|production-eu|Read-only local command; a friendly name is not proof of the underlying endpoint.
List Pods|kubectl get pods -n NAMESPACE -o wide|Lists Pods with readiness, restarts, IP and node placement.|Compare affected replicas and shared nodes.|READY is ready containers over total; STATUS is summarized state, and NODE shows placement.|NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS AGE IP NODE|Read-only; replace NAMESPACE with the intended scope.
Describe Pod|kubectl describe pod POD -n NAMESPACE|Shows Pod configuration, conditions, container state and events.|Distinguish scheduling, image, probe and storage failures.|Read Conditions, Last State and Events together; Warning reasons often point to the next check.|Warning FailedScheduling ...|Read-only; output may include sensitive configuration metadata.
Current logs|kubectl logs POD -n NAMESPACE --tail=100 --timestamps|Reads a bounded log tail with timestamps.|Correlate application errors with the incident.|Each line is application output; use -c CONTAINER when selection is ambiguous.|2026-... startup error ...|Read-only; redact credentials and personal data before sharing.
Previous container logs|kubectl logs POD -n NAMESPACE --previous --tail=100|Reads the previous terminated container instance's available logs.|Find the error that caused a restart rather than only current startup messages.|A previous instance must exist and logs must still be retained.|fatal: configuration could not be loaded|Read-only; specify -c for multi-container Pods.
Recent events|kubectl get events -n NAMESPACE --sort-by=.lastTimestamp|Lists retained namespace events ordered by last timestamp.|Build a controller-level incident timeline.|Reason and Message identify observations; repeated events may be aggregated and timestamps have limitations.|Warning FailedMount ...|Read-only; event retention is limited, so absence is not proof of no failure.
Service definition|kubectl describe service SERVICE -n NAMESPACE|Shows selectors, ports and service metadata.|Check whether routing selects the intended Pods and target port.|Selector must match labels; Port and TargetPort have different roles.|Selector: app=web ; Port: 80 ; TargetPort: 8080|Read-only; service existence does not prove backend readiness.
Service endpoints|kubectl get endpointslices -n NAMESPACE -l kubernetes.io/service-name=SERVICE -o yaml|Shows EndpointSlices associated with one Service.|Verify backend addresses and readiness conditions.|Inspect addresses, ports and conditions.ready for each endpoint.|conditions: ready: true|Read-only; EndpointSlices may include not-ready or terminating endpoints.
Rollout status|kubectl rollout status deployment/APP -n NAMESPACE --timeout=60s|Observes deployment progress with a bounded wait.|Detect a stalled rollout without waiting indefinitely.|Success means controller rollout completion; a timeout requires Pod and Deployment inspection.|deployment "APP" successfully rolled out|Read-only observation; still verify user-facing behavior.
Resource usage|kubectl top pods -n NAMESPACE|Shows recent Pod CPU and memory measurements.|Compare consumption across replicas and with configured limits.|CPU is commonly shown in millicores and memory in binary units; this is not a historical peak.|NAME CPU(cores) MEMORY(bytes)|Read-only; requires a functioning metrics API.
Permission check|kubectl auth can-i get pods -n NAMESPACE|Asks whether the current identity may perform the specified operation.|Distinguish authorization failures from network or authentication errors.|yes applies to that verb, resource and scope, not unrestricted cluster access.|yes|Read-only authorization review; impersonation options require separate privileges.
Storage claims|kubectl get pvc -n NAMESPACE|Lists persistent-volume claims and binding status.|Identify unbound storage blocking a workload.|STATUS Bound means a volume association exists; it does not prove attachment, mount or data health.|NAME STATUS VOLUME CAPACITY ACCESS MODES STORAGECLASS|Read-only; inspect claim events and storage topology next if Pending.
Available contexts|kubectl config get-contexts|Lists kubeconfig contexts and their cluster, identity and namespace associations.|Confirm the intended target before selecting a context.|The asterisk marks the current context; the namespace column can be empty, meaning default behavior.|CURRENT NAME CLUSTER AUTHINFO NAMESPACE|Read-only local configuration inspection; do not assume a familiar name proves production identity.
API discovery|kubectl api-resources --namespaced=true|Lists discoverable namespaced resource types.|Find the API name and short names for an unfamiliar object.|APIVERSION gives group/version; KIND is the object kind. Availability depends on the server and permissions.|NAME SHORTNAMES APIVERSION NAMESPACED KIND|Read-only; requires cluster discovery access.
Field documentation|kubectl explain deployment.spec.strategy|Displays schema documentation for a resource field.|Check supported rollout strategy fields before editing a manifest.|FIELD and DESCRIPTION explain the selected schema path; nested FIELDS list valid properties.|KIND: Deployment ... FIELD: strategy|Read-only; schema follows the server API version, not every controller-specific behavior.
Node placement view|kubectl get nodes -o wide|Lists nodes with status, versions and addresses.|Identify which node or version group is involved in a failure.|STATUS summarizes readiness; INTERNAL-IP and VERSION help correlate the affected node. Ready is not proof of all workload health.|NAME STATUS ROLES AGE VERSION INTERNAL-IP|Read-only; node addresses may be sensitive infrastructure information.
Node pressure detail|kubectl describe node NODE|Displays node conditions, capacity, allocations and events.|Investigate scheduling failures or node-level evictions.|Conditions identify pressure; Allocated resources summarizes requests and limits, not current usage.|Conditions: MemoryPressure=True|Read-only; replace NODE and correlate timestamps with the incident.
Deployment manifest view|kubectl get deployment APP -n NAMESPACE -o yaml|Retrieves the live Deployment specification and status.|Compare desired rollout settings with the object actually stored in the API.|spec is desired configuration; status reports controller observations. managedFields can be verbose.|spec: replicas: 3|Read-only; output can include configuration values, so redact before sharing.
Rollout revisions|kubectl rollout history deployment/APP -n NAMESPACE|Lists retained Deployment rollout revisions.|Establish which revision is available before considering recovery.|REVISION is rollout history, not a Git commit; change-cause may be absent.|REVISION CHANGE-CAUSE|Read-only; retained history depends on controller settings.
Review manifest changes|kubectl diff -f deployment.yaml|Compares the proposed manifest with live resources.|Review additions, removals and field changes before applying.|Diff prefixes identify changed lines; exit 0 means no differences, 1 means differences, and higher values signal errors.|- replicas: 2 / + replicas: 3|Does not persist the objects, but contacts the API and may invoke dry-run admission; protect secret-bearing diffs.
Server validation|kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f deployment.yaml|Runs server-side validation and dry-run admission without persisting the change.|Check whether the intended API server accepts a manifest.|A dry-run success means this request was accepted at that moment; it does not demonstrate runtime readiness.|deployment.apps/api configured (server dry run)|Requires applicable permissions; confirm context and webhook dry-run support.
Apply reviewed manifest|kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml|Creates or updates resources from a manifest using apply semantics.|Deploy an already reviewed desired configuration.|created, configured or unchanged describe the API operation; follow with rollout and service checks.|deployment.apps/api configured|Mutates the cluster. Review diff, namespace, field ownership and recovery procedure first.
Scale replicas|kubectl scale deployment/APP -n NAMESPACE --replicas=5|Changes a workload's desired replica count.|Perform an approved capacity adjustment.|The scaled message confirms the request, not five ready replicas; inspect rollout and available capacity afterward.|deployment.apps/api scaled|Mutates desired state; HPA or GitOps reconciliation may overwrite this setting.
Rollback revision|kubectl rollout undo deployment/APP -n NAMESPACE --to-revision=REVISION|Requests restoration of a retained Deployment revision.|Recover from a verified bad rollout when the old version remains compatible.|rolled back confirms the update request; monitor rollout status and user impact to verify recovery.|deployment.apps/api rolled back|Mutates workload version; inspect history, database compatibility and external configuration first.
Restart deployment|kubectl rollout restart deployment/APP -n NAMESPACE|Changes the Pod template annotation to trigger a fresh rollout.|Restart Pods after an approved configuration or operational change.|restarted means the template changed; readiness and availability still depend on the rollout strategy.|deployment.apps/api restarted|Mutates workload state and can disrupt traffic; inspect capacity and rollout settings first.
HPA conditions|kubectl describe hpa HPA -n NAMESPACE|Shows autoscaler targets, observed metrics and decision conditions.|Diagnose why replicas do not follow demand.|Compare current/target metrics with min/max replicas; Conditions and Events explain unavailable metrics or limits.|ScalingActive False FailedGetResourceMetric|Read-only; metrics availability and resource requests affect CPU utilization calculations.
Quota usage|kubectl describe resourcequota -n NAMESPACE|Lists namespace quota usage against hard limits.|Explain admission failures when new objects exceed the namespace budget.|Used and Hard compare consumed and permitted quantities by resource.|requests.cpu 8 8|Read-only; quota is different from available node capacity.
Limit defaults|kubectl get limitrange -n NAMESPACE -o yaml|Displays namespace defaults and constraints on resource settings.|Find values applied or enforced when workloads omit requests or limits.|default and defaultRequest are injected defaults; min/max constrain permitted values.|type: Container defaultRequest: cpu: 100m|Read-only; inspect actual admitted Pod settings as well.
Storage provisioning detail|kubectl describe pvc CLAIM -n NAMESPACE|Shows claim status, selected storage class and provisioning events.|Investigate Pending claims and mount dependencies.|Events can identify topology, provisioning or capacity issues; Bound does not prove a successful application mount.|Status: Pending ... WaitForFirstConsumer|Read-only; avoid deleting a claim as a diagnostic shortcut.
Storage class settings|kubectl get storageclass -o yaml|Lists provisioners and storage provisioning policies.|Check binding mode, reclaim policy and expansion support.|volumeBindingMode affects timing; allowVolumeExpansion is only one prerequisite for growth.|volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer|Read-only; backend and CSI driver support must also be checked.
Network policy scope|kubectl get networkpolicy -n NAMESPACE -o yaml|Displays policy selectors, peers, ports and policy types.|Review which Pods and traffic directions a policy covers.|podSelector selects destination policy subjects; ingress/egress describe allowed rules. Multiple policies combine additively.|policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]|Read-only; enforcement requires a supporting network plugin.
Namespace permissions|kubectl get rolebindings -n NAMESPACE -o yaml|Shows bindings between identities and namespaced role references.|Investigate how a workload receives API permissions.|subjects names identities; roleRef names a Role or ClusterRole. Read the referenced rules and other bindings too.|roleRef: kind: Role name: reader|Read-only; this is not a complete effective-permission calculation by itself.
Pod placement field|kubectl get pod POD -n NAMESPACE -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}'|Prints the assigned node field from one Pod.|Distinguish unscheduled work from node-side startup trouble.|A node name means assignment exists; empty output can mean the scheduler has not assigned a node.|worker-3|Read-only; no trailing newline may be printed by JSONPath output.
Inspect inside a container|kubectl exec POD -n NAMESPACE -c CONTAINER -- printenv APP_MODE|Runs a command inside a chosen container.|Check one non-secret runtime setting in the actual application environment.|Everything after -- is the container command; stdout is its output and absence may mean the variable is unset.|production|Exec can perform arbitrary changes with container permissions. This example reads one value; do not dump all secrets.
Local service tunnel|kubectl port-forward -n NAMESPACE service/SERVICE 8080:80 --address=127.0.0.1|Forwards a local port through the API connection to a selected backend.|Test a service endpoint without publishing a new external listener.|8080 is local and 80 is the Service port. Forwarding output confirms setup, not application correctness.|Forwarding from 127.0.0.1:8080 -> 8080|Creates a live local tunnel and requires permissions; stop with Ctrl-C. It does not test the normal external network path.
Temporary debug container|kubectl debug -n NAMESPACE POD -it --image=APPROVED_DEBUG_IMAGE --target=CONTAINER|Adds an ephemeral debugging container to a running Pod.|Inspect a minimal workload that lacks diagnostic tools.|The image must provide suitable tools; --target requests the target process namespace when supported by the runtime.|Defaulting debug container name to debugger-...|Mutates the Pod's ephemeral-container configuration; use an approved image and scoped incident access.
Disruption budget detail|kubectl describe pdb BUDGET -n NAMESPACE|Displays healthy counts and permitted voluntary disruptions.|Understand an eviction blocked by a PodDisruptionBudget.|Allowed disruptions is the current eviction allowance; healthy counts depend on selected Pods.|Allowed disruptions: 0|Read-only; a PDB does not guarantee protection from involuntary node failures.
Cordon node|kubectl cordon NODE|Marks a node unschedulable for ordinary new scheduling.|Prepare a node for an approved maintenance workflow.|cordoned confirms the node flag; existing Pods are not evicted by this command.|node/worker-3 cordoned|Mutates scheduling availability; verify spare capacity and the intended node first.
Drain with eviction|kubectl drain NODE --ignore-daemonsets --timeout=10m|Evicts eligible Pods from a node while respecting normal eviction controls.|Move workloads away before planned node maintenance.|Errors identify blockers such as PDBs, unmanaged Pods or local data. A timeout is not proof the node is empty.|node/worker-3 drained|Disruptive operation: review capacity, PDBs and local storage. Do not add force or data-deletion flags to bypass unexplained blockers.
Restore scheduling|kubectl uncordon NODE|Clears the node's unschedulable flag.|Return a verified healthy node to scheduling after maintenance.|uncordoned means scheduling is enabled; it does not prove all node services or workload paths are healthy.|node/worker-3 uncordoned|Mutates scheduling state; complete node-health checks before returning capacity.
